Input: {"question": "When was Attorney General Russell called to the Bar?", "source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d00", "start": 0, "end": 131, "text": "became at once the champion of the Catholics who had resisted organised attempts at proselytising by Protestants in these counties."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d00", "start": 132, "end": 204, "text": "He matriculated at Trinity College, Dublin in 1856, but never graduated."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d00", "start": 205, "end": 336, "text": "His success was so striking that his legal friends urged him to become a barrister in London, and in 1856 he entered Lincoln's Inn."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d00", "start": 337, "end": 480, "text": "Having followed an extensive course by close private study under the direction of Maine, Broom, and Birkbeck, he was called to the Bar in 1859."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d00", "start": 481, "end": 589, "text": "His success on the northern circuit soon recalled him to London, where he took silk in 1872, and divided the"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d01", "start": 0, "end": 13, "text": "for the law ."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d01", "start": 14, "end": 163, "text": "Having gained his degree at Trinity College , Dublin , he was admitted as a solicitor in 1854 and practised in Belfast for the next couple of years ."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d01", "start": 164, "end": 230, "text": "In 1856 , he moved to London and was admitted at Lincoln ’ s Inn ."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d01", "start": 231, "end": 305, "text": "Obviously good at his calling , he was called to the Bar in January 1859 ."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d01", "start": 306, "end": 475, "text": "He continued to progress : by 1872 , he was a QC on the Northern Circuit , where his oratorical vehemence led to his advocacy being described as an “ elemental force ” ."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d01", "start": 476, "end": 481, "text": "While"}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d02", "start": 0, "end": 5, "text": "1885."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d02", "start": 6, "end": 174, "text": "His success on the northern circuit soon recalled him to London, where he took silk in 1872, [1] and divided the mercantile business of the circuit with Lord Herschell."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d02", "start": 175, "end": 423, "text": "The increasing demand for his services may be judged by his fees which averaged £3000 a year from 1862 to 1872, £10,000 in the next decade, £16,000 in the third, and in 1893–1894, his last year of practice (while Attorney-General), reached £32,826."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d02", "start": 424, "end": 491, "text": "His knowledge of law, business, and human character, a flexible and"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d03", "start": 0, "end": 116, "text": "Early boyhood onwards he maintained a keen interest in politics, and pre-eminently in the public affairs of Ireland."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d03", "start": 117, "end": 193, "text": "In 1845 he was called to the Bar, marking the beginning of his legal career."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d03", "start": 194, "end": 335, "text": "In 1859 he published a pamphlet entitled The Catholic in the Workhouse, and an article from his pen is to be found in The Dublin Review, vol."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d03", "start": 336, "end": 343, "text": "xlviii."}, "D4S5": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d03", "start": 344, "end": 346, "text": "p."}, "D4S6": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d03", "start": 347, "end": 351, "text": "497."}, "D4S7": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d03", "start": 352, "end": 415, "text": "His legal work was not wholly confined to the north of England."}, "D4S8": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d03", "start": 416, "end": 511, "text": "He was employed at the Guildhall and elsewhere by solicitors of position in the City of London."}, "D4S9": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d03", "start": 512, "end": 578, "text": "He was one of the counsel engaged in the Windham lunacy case in..."}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d04", "start": 0, "end": 9, "text": "Leslie W."}, "D5S2": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d04", "start": 10, "end": 68, "text": "Russell Leslie Wead Russell (April 15, 1840 in Canton, St."}, "D5S3": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d04", "start": 69, "end": 188, "text": "Lawrence County, New York – February 3, 1903 in Flushing, Queens, New York City) was an American lawyer and politician."}, "D5S4": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d04", "start": 189, "end": 285, "text": "He was the son of Assemblyman John Leslie Russell (1805–1861) and Mary Sybil (Wead) Russell (ca."}, "D5S5": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d04", "start": 286, "end": 297, "text": "1812–1870)."}, "D5S6": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d04", "start": 298, "end": 369, "text": "He was educated at Canton Academy, and at age 16 began to teach school."}, "D5S7": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d04", "start": 370, "end": 435, "text": "Then he studied law at Albany, New York and Milwaukee, Wisconsin."}, "D5S8": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d04", "start": 436, "end": 509, "text": "He was admitted to the bar in 1861, and commenced practice in Canton, NY."}, "D5S9": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d04", "start": 510, "end": 585, "text": "On October 19, 1864, he married Harriet Jane Lawrence (1843–1931), and they"}, "D6S1": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d05", "start": 0, "end": 21, "text": "Warrington Guardian ."}, "D6S2": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d05", "start": 22, "end": 41, "text": "Press Association ."}, "D6S3": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d05", "start": 42, "end": 57, "text": "26 March 2015 ."}, "D6S4": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d05", "start": 58, "end": 102, "text": "Archived from the original on 2 April 2015 ."}, "D6S5": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d05", "start": 103, "end": 126, "text": "Retrieved 4 June 2015 ."}, "D6S6": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d05", "start": 127, "end": 183, "text": "^ `` Russell Brand donates Trew Era cafe to charity '' ."}, "D6S7": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d05", "start": 184, "end": 194, "text": "BBC News ."}, "D6S8": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d05", "start": 195, "end": 214, "text": "25 September 2016 ."}, "D6S9": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d05", "start": 215, "end": 260, "text": "Archived from the original on 15 March 2022 ."}, "D6S10": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d05", "start": 261, "end": 286, "text": "Retrieved 15 March 2022 ."}, "D6S11": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0233-94c581b73f81:d05", "start": 287, "end": 358,